## std::map — `Hash[K, V]` method surface (Ruby's Hash).
##
## Zero-Rust-stdlib migration, Phase 3 (Option C). `class Hash[K, V]`
## below carries BOTH the FFI alias decls (`Hash_<m>` → `ruxen_hash_<m>`,
## consumed by `Lowerer::resolve_ffi_alias_callee`) AND the TRUE surface
## return types delegated via the builtin-receiver bridge
## (`bridge_builtin_method`, reached for `Ty::Map(_,_)` because
## `builtin_bridge::is_delegated_head` now includes the Map head).
##
## `Ty::Map` Displays as `Hash[K, V]`, so the call-site mangling and the
## method-home key both use `"Hash"`: `method_home_key(Ty::Map) → "Hash"`
## and `substitute_generics_in_return`'s synthetic name is `"Hash"`, so
## the class's declared `[K, V]` substitute against the receiver's
## concrete key/value types.
##
## `Hash` is NOT in `register_builtins`'s type-scope map — `let h:
## Hash[K, V]` resolves via the hardcoded arm in `resolve_type_expr`
## that builds `Ty::Map(_, _)` directly. The class below homes the
## METHODS without touching the receiver TYPE.
##
## ## ABI notes
##
## Every method's true surface type is ABI-identical to its C symbol's
## wire width: `void*` (`get`/`remove`, boxed `Option`) / `RuxenVec*`
## (`keys`/`values`/`to_a`) / `RuxenHash*` (`new`/`with_capacity`) cross
## the C ABI as I64, `uint64_t` (len) as I64 (= `USize`), `int8_t`
## (`contains_key`) as I8 (= `Bool`). The parity guard
## `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins the widths.
##
## No residuals — every Hash method has a real C symbol and a statically
## substitutable return.
##
## The internal `Ty::Map` variant and the `ruxen_hash_*` runtime
## symbols keep their legacy names; only the surface spelling is
## Ruby (`Hash`, never the Rust `Map` / `HashMap`).
class Hash[K, V]
  lib "../hash/runtime/hash.c"
    def self.new as "ruxen_hash_new" -> Hash[K, V]
    def self.with_capacity as "ruxen_hash_with_capacity"(cap: Int) -> Hash[K, V]
    def size as "ruxen_hash_len" -> USize
    def empty? as "ruxen_hash_is_empty" -> Bool
    def get as "ruxen_hash_get"(key: K) -> Option[&V]
    def key? as "ruxen_hash_contains_key"(key: K) -> Bool
    def keys as "ruxen_hash_keys" -> Array[&K]
    def values as "ruxen_hash_values" -> Array[&V]
    def to_a as "ruxen_hash_entries" -> Array[(K, V)]
    def insert as "ruxen_hash_insert"(key: K, value: V)
    def remove as "ruxen_hash_remove"(key: K) -> Option[V]
    def clear as "ruxen_hash_clear"
  end

  # The combinator family (`map`/`select`/`reject`/`find`/`reduce`/
  # `all?`/`any?`/`partition`/…) comes from the shared `mixin
  # Enumerable[T]` over the single `each` primitive below. Hash's
  # ELEMENT is the `(K, V)` pair, so it includes `Enumerable[(K, V)]`:
  # every combinator closure receives a `(K, V)` tuple (tuple-field
  # access `kv.0` / `kv.1` is sound — Bug 3, `604_closure_tuple_param_field`).
  include Enumerable[(K, V)]

  # Generic `each`: yield every `(K, V)` entry to the user closure. The
  # `.rx` body is lowered ONCE as the opaque `Hash_each` (K/V abstract);
  # it materializes the entries via the FFI call on `self` (`self.to_a`)
  # and forwards each tuple to `f`. Proves the FFI-on-self result flows
  # out of the opaque body into the iteration.
  def each(f: any Fn[Fn((K, V)) -> nil]) -> nil
    let entries = self.to_a
    entries.each do |kv| f.(kv) end
    nil
  end
end
